HOTEL-APP:
    create reservation, list reservation, edit reservation, delete reservation
    CRUD

App root -> router -> (home component)
                |-> (reservation form component, Reservation list component) <-> (Reservation service)

ROOTER OUTLET:
-> remove everything except <router-outlet></router-outlet> in app.component.html
- shows whatever path we have navigated to
- by using router we can display various components by assighihng routes to it.
app-routing.module.ts:
const routes: Routes = [
  {path: "",component: HomeComponent},
  {path: "list",component: ReservationListComponent},
  {path: "new",component: ReservationFormComponent}
];

Module:
- "ng generate module home" / "ng g m .."
- generate home component in home module
    "ng g c home --module=home"
- a component can have only one module
- we need to provide all modules that we create under main app.module.ts

Service:
creates reservation service under reservation folder
"ng generate service reservation/reservation"

create reservation interface:
"ng g i models/reservation"

-------------------------------
NAVIGATION MENU:

<div>
    <h2> Welcome to Hotel  Reservation Portal </h2>
    <button [routerLink]="['/new']">Create a new reservation</button>
    <button [routerLink]="['/list']"> View all Reservations</button>
</div>

- to use the routerLinnk we need to import it to our module
"import { RouterModule } from '@angular/router';" home.module.ts

--------------------------------
RSERVATION FORM:
- we are using form module , so import it 
"import { FormsModule, ReactiveFormsModule } from '@angular/forms';" reservation.module.ts
ReactiveFormsModule:
    Validate Form in Typescript class
Template-Driven Form Validation:
    Validate Form in HTML Template

reservation-form.component.ts:
"import { FormBuilder, FormGroup, Validators } from '@angular/forms';"

- form input needs to have formControlName , so that we can use form group and form builder to grab the value of i/p field
    <input type="date" formControlName="checkInDate">

FORM VALIDATION:
    - FromBuilder -> groups all i/p fields together
    - add validations Oninit() of the component
    - Dependency Injection: we have system in Angulat tha t says i know ur reservation component want to have FormBuilder,
        Angular DJ does that, create a FromBuilder instance and submits it to the constructor
        constructor(private fromBuilder:FormBuilder, ){}
        ngOnInit(): void {
            this.reservationForm = this.fromBuilder.group({
            /* names shd be same the one we picked for formControlName in form*/
            checkInDate: ['',Validators.required],
            checkOutDate: ['',Validators.required],
            guestName: ['',Validators.required],
            guestEmail: ['',[Validators.required, Validators.email]],
            roomNumber: ['',Validators.required],
            })
        }

Dynamic toggle html attributes based on form state:
- Disable submit if form is invalid
<button type="Submit" [disabled]="reservationForm.invalid">Submit</button>

Validation messages:
- "ngIf" directive to create if statements
- if the input is invalid and its touched(don't show from the beginning, dhow msg once its been touched/accessed)
<div *ngIf="reservationForm.get('checkInDate')?.invalid && reservationForm.get('checkInDate')?.touched">
            CheckIn data is required</div>

-------------------------------------
RESERVATION SERVICE:
- place where we want to handle the data (CRUD OPERATIONS)
-> @Inejactable makes the reservation service inject into a constructor. 
- Where ever we need reservation service we can simply go into the constructor of another class and 
     use angular dependeny injection to inject instance of reservation service
        @Injectable({
            providedIn: 'root'
        })
        export class ReservationService {
            //CRUD OPS
        }

--------------------------------------
RESERVATION LIST:
show the list of reservations using getReservations from ReservationService

ngtemplate and localReference:
- ng template doesnt get rendered by default , it needs to get activated. 
Here we are using local Reference noReservation which gets activated when there reservations.length =0
        <ul *ngIf="reservations.length; else noReservation">
            <li *ngFor="let reservation of reservations">...</li>
        </ul>

        <ng-template #noReservation>
            <p>No Reservation Available</p>
        </ng-template>

---------------------------------------

Router Navigate method to redirect the user:
    import { Router } from '@angular/router';
    constructor(
            private router: Router
        ){}
    onSubmit(){
        ...
      this.router.navigate(['list'])
    }

